@startuml component_states

title Component State Machine

state Terminated : no resource is running
state Starting : waiting for the ready condition
state Ready : resource has finished its startup
state Terminating {
  state GracefulShutdown : waiting up to the shutdown timeout
  state ForcedShutdown : waiting up to kMaxSigKillDelay

  [*] --> GracefulShutdown
  GracefulShutdown -[#red]-> ForcedShutdown : <color:red>shutdown timeout expired</color>\n<color:red>/ force_stop_action</color>
}

[*] -d-> Terminated

Terminated -down-> Starting : activate() / start_action
Starting --> Ready : ready condition met / kSuccess
Starting -[#red]-> Terminating : <color:red>activation failed / stop_action</color>
note on link
  Activation fails if
  * the resource terminated unexpectedly during startup
  * the ready condition timed out
  * the ready condition failed, e.g. resource terminated or
    activation got cancelled
  * supervision could not be activated
end note
Terminated -down-> Starting : retry [retries left, not cancelled]\n/ start_action

Ready --> Terminating : deactivate()\n/stop_action
Ready -up[#red]-> Terminated : <color:red>unexpected termination</color>

Terminating -up-> Terminated : terminated
ForcedShutdown -up[#red]-> Terminated : <color:red>kMaxSigKillDelay expired / gave up</color>

legend bottom
  activate() and deactivate() never run at the same time.
  A failed activation is retried up to activation_retry_attempts times; afterwards activate() fails.
  A self-terminating resource exiting with 0 is expected and keeps the component Ready.
  Every failure ends in Terminated, so the component can always be activated again.
endlegend

@enduml
